From 22c7647fde05dd0151e3fe343323bc2ee8bb5a74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:54:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Preprocessor: the output given back before the backend (#628)

Once the checker has checked the program and given its warnings, the
backend reads the program: only a diagnostic (a token's place and
spelling) and -g's line table read the preprocessor's output, 10 bytes
a token, which on sqlite3.c is 12.5 MB of the backend's peak, the
compile's.

A unit without errors whose history is still deferred, going on to its
object or executable alone, now gives the output's columns back before
the backend (pp_release_output), keeping the count. A reader asks for
them first (pp_output_ensure): a diagnostic records the history, whose
replay, the run again, gives them back with its count checked
(pp_output_adopt); a reader that doesn't record the history, as the
undefined reference's system-header test, has them from a replay
without history, so that the unrecorded history reads as before. A
replay that fails leaves columns of zeros, which place the diagnostics
after its fatal error nowhere. Units with -g, --scalar or
-flazy-system-headers record their history and keep the output, as do
a unit with diagnostics and one that links with others. With
--poison-arenas the columns stay mapped but unreadable.

test-preprocessor's deferred history test releases the output of every
fixture's deferred run, has it back without history and compares it
token for token, then releases it again for the probe's diagnostic,
whose replay gives it back. Run with every unit's output poisoned,
test-checker-driver's four parts, test-check-units, test-check-lanes,
test-checker-deferred, test-checker and c-testsuite find no reader
that doesn't ask.

On the VM, -c sqlite3.c's peak is 251,968 -> 239,848 KiB, with the
same objects with and without -Wall -Wextra, -g and --lanes 8.

